WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.556 --> 00:00:11.916
Well today I'm really glad
to get to share something with you

00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:15.036
that I've been working on
for the past 15 years.

00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:16.996
But more importantly,

00:00:17.036 --> 00:00:20.196
something that could become
the first specimen

00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:24.556
of an entire new industry
that we might find all around the planet

00:00:24.596 --> 00:00:26.036
over the next decades.

00:00:27.196 --> 00:00:30.796
Think of it as something
like the very first smartphone.

00:00:30.836 --> 00:00:35.876
Still quite clumsy and quite expensive,
but of transformative technology.

00:00:36.396 --> 00:00:40.196
Smartphones have changed
the way how we communicate.

00:00:41.036 --> 00:00:42.996
This, I hope,

00:00:42.996 --> 00:00:46.076
could change the way
we deal with global warming.

00:00:46.076 --> 00:00:47.276
This is Orca.

00:00:47.316 --> 00:00:51.756
This is the first worldwide commercial
direct air capture and storage plant.

00:00:51.796 --> 00:00:53.036
It is in Iceland,

00:00:53.036 --> 00:00:57.476
and it is an industrial plant
that extracts CO2 out of ambient air.

00:00:57.516 --> 00:01:00.356
We have operated now
for more than one year.

00:01:00.716 --> 00:01:03.796
It costs more than 10 million
dollars to build Orca.

00:01:04.676 --> 00:01:06.516
And its modules,

00:01:06.556 --> 00:01:09.716
those eight boxes
that we call CO2 collectors,

00:01:09.756 --> 00:01:14.116
they are designed to extract a bit more
than 10 tons of carbon dioxide

00:01:14.156 --> 00:01:15.876
from the air every day.

00:01:16.316 --> 00:01:18.036
It sounds quite expensive.

00:01:18.516 --> 00:01:22.076
However, when thinking about cost,
we should keep in mind one thing.

00:01:22.116 --> 00:01:24.716
There is one thing that we cannot buy,

00:01:24.716 --> 00:01:26.076
and that is time.

00:01:26.636 --> 00:01:28.436
And we need to be quite fast here.

00:01:30.236 --> 00:01:32.596
So that is why

00:01:32.636 --> 00:01:35.796
Orca is not there to demonstrate costs.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:42.716
Orca is there to show in the field,
out there, in the weather,

00:01:42.756 --> 00:01:45.596
that the plant is operating,
and Orca is working.

00:01:46.236 --> 00:01:48.716
So the next step for us at Climeworks

00:01:48.716 --> 00:01:50.836
and for all the other
companies in the field

00:01:50.836 --> 00:01:54.076
is now going forward,
improving the technology,

00:01:54.116 --> 00:01:58.436
scaling it up from tens to hundreds
to millions of tons capacity.

00:01:58.796 --> 00:02:02.676
And as we go ahead and we scale it up,

00:02:02.716 --> 00:02:05.916
with every new plant going online,

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:10.316
we'll have efficiencies going up
and we'll have costs going down.

00:02:10.956 --> 00:02:13.076
Now, how does it work?

00:02:13.396 --> 00:02:14.796
In the case of Orca,

00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:19.636
we have fans mounted to the CO2 collectors
that draw air through them.

00:02:19.956 --> 00:02:23.476
Inside there is a filter material
that we call sorbent,

00:02:23.516 --> 00:02:25.196
a highly porous material.

00:02:25.636 --> 00:02:27.196
And as the air pulls through it,

00:02:27.196 --> 00:02:30.996
the CO2 is bound at the surface
of this material.

00:02:31.036 --> 00:02:35.276
Think of it as a sponge
that sucks up water in its pores.

00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:39.476
We do that for a certain time until the
material is filled with used CO2,

00:02:39.516 --> 00:02:40.716
it is saturated.

00:02:40.756 --> 00:02:42.436
And we close the modules,

00:02:42.436 --> 00:02:45.156
we heat them up
to around 100 degrees Celsius,

00:02:45.196 --> 00:02:48.316
and we can extract
concentrated CO2 from them.

00:02:49.076 --> 00:02:51.676
The good thing is
that the main energy input

00:02:51.716 --> 00:02:54.436
that we need for that process
is low temperature heat

00:02:54.476 --> 00:02:56.636
at around 100 degrees Celsius.

00:02:57.196 --> 00:03:00.636
And it can entirely be powered
by renewable energy.

00:03:02.076 --> 00:03:06.716
Now that looks quite easy
at first glance, right?

00:03:06.716 --> 00:03:09.516
And indeed, the process
behind that is quite simple.

00:03:10.196 --> 00:03:14.276
However, when it comes
to implementing that into reality

00:03:14.316 --> 00:03:15.516
in the field out there

00:03:15.556 --> 00:03:18.796
with the purpose of having a plant
that is constantly operating,

00:03:18.836 --> 00:03:21.316
24/7, reliably,

00:03:21.356 --> 00:03:23.476
then it becomes quite challenging.

00:03:23.996 --> 00:03:26.436
Let me give you two examples
why it is a challenge

00:03:26.476 --> 00:03:29.516
to capture and remove CO2 from the air.

00:03:29.516 --> 00:03:30.676
First of all,

00:03:30.716 --> 00:03:33.236
there is not a lot of CO2 in the air.

00:03:33.276 --> 00:03:36.356
We're currently at around 420 ppm.

00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:42.716
That means one molecule out of 2,500
molecules in the air around us

00:03:42.716 --> 00:03:43.916
is CO2.

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:45.196
That's not a lot.

00:03:45.196 --> 00:03:49.276
And that means to extract
only one ton of CO2 from the air,

00:03:49.316 --> 00:03:53.316
we need to filter around
two million cubic meters of air.

00:03:53.356 --> 00:03:55.596
That's about 800 Olympic swimming pools.

00:03:56.316 --> 00:04:00.396
And there's another way of picturing that,
which I personally find quite puzzling.

00:04:00.436 --> 00:04:02.116
Imagine one of these filters

00:04:02.156 --> 00:04:05.716
with an inlet area
of one meter by one meter.

00:04:05.756 --> 00:04:07.196
And the amount of air,

00:04:07.236 --> 00:04:08.916
like, think of it as a block of air

00:04:08.956 --> 00:04:12.076
that you have to push
through that filter inlet

00:04:12.076 --> 00:04:15.316
to take just one ton
of CO2 out of the air,

00:04:15.356 --> 00:04:17.476
is 2,000 kilometers long.

00:04:19.356 --> 00:04:22.556
Another example why this is a challenge

00:04:22.556 --> 00:04:26.196
is that it is not so easy to find good,

00:04:26.196 --> 00:04:28.076
actually, the best sorbent materials,

00:04:28.116 --> 00:04:30.396
which are both high-performing

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:34.516
and which can prevail outside
there in the field with a long life.

00:04:34.836 --> 00:04:38.396
Now, in this next picture,
you see Christoph and myself,

00:04:38.436 --> 00:04:39.716
cofounders of Climeworks,

00:04:39.756 --> 00:04:42.716
in the very early days
of Climeworks, working on that.

00:04:42.756 --> 00:04:46.036
The very first sorbents,
we produced them with our own hands,

00:04:46.076 --> 00:04:49.436
such as you would do your laundry
if you didn't have a washing machine.

00:04:49.476 --> 00:04:51.876
We set some sorbents on fire,

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:55.596
and we produced hundreds of samples
in the past 10 years.

00:04:56.196 --> 00:04:57.516
Looking forward,

00:04:57.556 --> 00:05:00.236
thousands of more will come, very likely.

00:05:01.916 --> 00:05:04.636
Now, this is one way of doing it.

00:05:04.676 --> 00:05:07.636
We are doing that in our company,
but many others are doing it.

00:05:07.676 --> 00:05:09.636
There are companies out there, start-ups,

00:05:09.676 --> 00:05:13.116
more established companies
and researchers and scientists.

00:05:13.156 --> 00:05:14.356
And that is a good thing

00:05:14.356 --> 00:05:17.356
because the challenge
ahead of us is immense.

00:05:17.396 --> 00:05:21.036
Now let's step back
and look at the big picture.

00:05:21.076 --> 00:05:25.636
So where does this kind of technology fit
into our overall fight

00:05:25.636 --> 00:05:27.236
against climate change?

00:05:27.276 --> 00:05:29.636
And it's important
that first and foremost,

00:05:29.676 --> 00:05:33.916
the most important thing
we need to do is, and will always stay,

00:05:33.956 --> 00:05:37.916
the drastic reduction of emissions
of greenhouse gases.

00:05:38.316 --> 00:05:39.436
Without that,

00:05:39.476 --> 00:05:42.836
we will have no chance of ever meeting
any of our climate goals.

00:05:43.436 --> 00:05:44.796
Then in a second step,

00:05:44.836 --> 00:05:49.476
it will be important that we can all
ramp up the capacity

00:05:49.516 --> 00:05:52.516
of extracting and removing
CO2 from the atmosphere.

00:05:53.196 --> 00:05:56.876
Now, when thinking about the latter,
so far I've spoken about technology,

00:05:56.916 --> 00:06:00.556
but we shouldn't forget that also,
nature offers several solutions

00:06:00.556 --> 00:06:02.076
to extract carbon from the air,

00:06:02.116 --> 00:06:04.116
such as forests and oceans.

00:06:04.476 --> 00:06:06.396
And one element that is very important

00:06:06.396 --> 00:06:10.476
will be doubling down
on these methods offered by nature,

00:06:10.476 --> 00:06:13.156
enhancing them and protecting them.

00:06:13.476 --> 00:06:14.876
That is important.

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:19.716
However, will it be sufficient to rely
only on nature-based carbon sinks?

00:06:20.116 --> 00:06:21.436
Very likely not.

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:25.276
Trees need time to grow
and forests need space,

00:06:25.316 --> 00:06:28.316
and there is simply
just not enough area available

00:06:28.356 --> 00:06:31.556
to plant entire continents of new forests,

00:06:31.596 --> 00:06:33.036
which we would need

00:06:33.076 --> 00:06:37.116
in order to capture many billions of tons
of CO2 from the atmosphere.

00:06:38.196 --> 00:06:39.356
And therefore,

00:06:39.396 --> 00:06:42.796
besides preserving all these
nature-based solutions and enhancing them,

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:47.076
it will be very important
to scale up technology-based solutions

00:06:47.116 --> 00:06:50.596
for large-scale carbon removal
from the atmosphere.

00:06:51.836 --> 00:06:55.316
Now, I often get asked if you build
direct air capture plants

00:06:55.316 --> 00:06:57.036
like the one we saw before,

00:06:57.036 --> 00:06:59.596
is that a license to continue polluting?

00:07:00.196 --> 00:07:01.716
It is clearly not.

00:07:01.716 --> 00:07:05.836
Direct air capture is not a silver bullet,
but it is a must-have.

00:07:06.556 --> 00:07:09.956
Had we stopped emitting 20, 30 years ago,

00:07:09.956 --> 00:07:12.436
or at least substantially
reduced our emissions,

00:07:12.476 --> 00:07:15.236
Climeworks and this whole
emerging industry around us

00:07:15.276 --> 00:07:17.076
would likely not be here today.

00:07:17.476 --> 00:07:18.636
And for the climate,

00:07:18.676 --> 00:07:20.956
that would probably
be the better solution.

00:07:21.356 --> 00:07:23.556
However, that's not where we are today.

00:07:24.156 --> 00:07:27.676
Today, the IPCC has indicated
in their last report

00:07:27.716 --> 00:07:31.556
that technology-based solutions
to remove carbon from the air

00:07:31.596 --> 00:07:33.316
are a necessity,

00:07:33.316 --> 00:07:36.116
and they included it [in] their models.

00:07:38.476 --> 00:07:42.116
And that is why this entire industry
that is currently emerging

00:07:42.116 --> 00:07:46.956
now needs to double down on moving
from small-scale and relatively expensive

00:07:46.996 --> 00:07:49.156
to larger-scale and cheap.

00:07:49.676 --> 00:07:52.316
Double down on developing, deploying,

00:07:52.356 --> 00:07:54.636
learning and adjusting in the field.

00:07:55.556 --> 00:07:56.996
We have to develop markets,

00:07:57.036 --> 00:07:59.156
we have to develop policy.

00:07:59.156 --> 00:08:02.516
And we have to develop mechanisms

00:08:02.516 --> 00:08:06.396
to analyze the CO2 capture

00:08:06.436 --> 00:08:09.516
and other technical ways
of removing CO2

00:08:09.556 --> 00:08:11.636
that take care of the fact

00:08:11.676 --> 00:08:14.276
that by taking CO2
out of the air with machines

00:08:14.316 --> 00:08:15.916
and putting it down in the ground

00:08:15.916 --> 00:08:19.996
we have one of the most permanent ways
of storing the CO2 away

00:08:20.036 --> 00:08:22.076
for millions of years.

00:08:22.676 --> 00:08:25.356
Now let’s get back one step,

00:08:25.356 --> 00:08:27.676
and let’s get back to today where we are.

00:08:27.716 --> 00:08:30.316
And let’s get back to the present,
to what is concrete.

00:08:30.316 --> 00:08:32.916
Let's get back to Iceland
and our Orca plant.

00:08:33.356 --> 00:08:37.476
I've told you before how these collectors
extract CO2 from the air.

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:41.356
Now, how do we make sure that it really
permanently stays locked away?

00:08:41.956 --> 00:08:43.716
For that reason, in Iceland,

00:08:43.756 --> 00:08:46.436
we have partnered up
with a company Carbfix

00:08:46.436 --> 00:08:48.276
and have developed a method

00:08:48.316 --> 00:08:51.396
for permanent carbon storage
through mineralization.

00:08:52.076 --> 00:08:55.556
What they do is they take the CO2,
mix it with water,

00:08:55.556 --> 00:09:00.596
and inject it into underground volcanic,
so-called basalt rock formations.

00:09:00.636 --> 00:09:05.436
That is one of the most abundant stone
or stone material that you find on Earth.

00:09:05.916 --> 00:09:07.116
And by doing so,

00:09:07.156 --> 00:09:09.476
the CO2 binds with the rock,
it mineralizes,

00:09:09.516 --> 00:09:13.116
and it literally turns into stone
within two years,

00:09:13.156 --> 00:09:15.196
hundreds of meters underground.

00:09:15.876 --> 00:09:18.756
The good thing about that method
is that it is very tangible,

00:09:18.756 --> 00:09:20.356
and it's very easy to understand.

00:09:20.356 --> 00:09:24.196
And for that purpose, I've brought
this rock sample here for you.

00:09:24.236 --> 00:09:28.556
That is taken actually from a drill core

00:09:28.556 --> 00:09:31.676
of a basalt rock formation
hundreds of meters underground.

00:09:31.716 --> 00:09:34.476
And what you can see here,
that's a black rock,

00:09:34.516 --> 00:09:37.476
which is the black basalt rock,
containing some white crystals.

00:09:37.516 --> 00:09:42.076
And these crystals are CO2
turned into stone underground

00:09:42.116 --> 00:09:43.396
and locked away.

00:09:43.876 --> 00:09:48.796
(Applause)

00:09:48.836 --> 00:09:52.076
Now I explained to you
one method of doing this.

00:09:52.116 --> 00:09:54.636
There are several other methods out there.

00:09:54.996 --> 00:09:57.596
There are several other techniques,
other technologies.

00:09:57.636 --> 00:10:00.716
But the important thing about it
is that this thing is out there.

00:10:00.716 --> 00:10:03.636
It's in practice, it's operating,
and we are learning from it.

00:10:03.676 --> 00:10:05.916
And that is so essential
for this new industry.

00:10:05.916 --> 00:10:07.876
And we have learned a lot, actually.

00:10:07.876 --> 00:10:11.996
We have learned how to operate
on hot, dry summer days.

00:10:12.716 --> 00:10:15.196
We learned to operate them
in pouring rain.

00:10:15.196 --> 00:10:18.476
And we even had to learn
how to operate them on a just normal day.

00:10:19.716 --> 00:10:22.516
We had to learn
how to ramp up the Orca plant

00:10:22.556 --> 00:10:25.916
immediately after an Icelandic snowstorm.

00:10:26.636 --> 00:10:28.916
And this all is what we need

00:10:28.956 --> 00:10:33.076
in order to deploy these plants
all over the world.

00:10:33.116 --> 00:10:35.036
We should deploy them all over the world

00:10:35.036 --> 00:10:37.156
because we do need so many of them.

00:10:37.196 --> 00:10:42.716
We can deploy them all over the world
because the air is everywhere around us

00:10:42.716 --> 00:10:46.756
and the CO2 is almost
the same concentration

00:10:46.796 --> 00:10:48.196
everywhere where we are.

00:10:48.236 --> 00:10:50.796
So it doesn't matter
where we capture the CO2.

00:10:51.236 --> 00:10:52.396
And for that reason,

00:10:52.436 --> 00:10:54.556
and in the spirit of deploying more,

00:10:54.596 --> 00:10:55.796
in June of this year

00:10:55.796 --> 00:10:59.116
we have announced the next step
on our Climeworks scale-up road map,

00:10:59.116 --> 00:11:01.836
which is named Mammoth,
and that is what you see here.

00:11:01.876 --> 00:11:06.116
That is a plant which is nine times
larger than the Orca plant,

00:11:06.156 --> 00:11:07.916
that will be in Iceland as well.

00:11:08.236 --> 00:11:12.396
And the first tons of CO2
will be captured by the end of 2023.

00:11:13.156 --> 00:11:18.116
This winter, we will finalize the first
building before the snow starts.

00:11:19.316 --> 00:11:22.396
Now there is a big challenge ahead of us.

00:11:23.436 --> 00:11:27.356
And actually, you can only really
understand what this challenge is

00:11:27.396 --> 00:11:28.916
and really quantify it

00:11:28.956 --> 00:11:31.476
if you build these things out there,

00:11:31.476 --> 00:11:33.596
make mistakes and learn from them.

00:11:34.156 --> 00:11:35.516
So it's a hard problem.

00:11:35.556 --> 00:11:37.876
It's not a simple problem to solve,

00:11:37.876 --> 00:11:39.676
and there is no easy solution to it.

00:11:39.716 --> 00:11:42.836
And without action like this,
there is no solution at all.

00:11:43.396 --> 00:11:47.316
And so when I started looking
back at the last 15 years,

00:11:47.316 --> 00:11:48.756
what we have been doing,

00:11:48.796 --> 00:11:52.436
I'm now looking forward
to the next 15 years that are to come

00:11:52.476 --> 00:11:54.076
because that is what we need.

00:11:54.116 --> 00:11:55.236
Thank you.

00:11:55.276 --> 00:11:59.356
(Applause)

